WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.090
Stage ten: coach in your pocket.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.718
At Camille's place, one machine stays on all night: the coach machine.

3
00:00:09.818 --> 00:00:16.860
It holds the files and the Garmin access, and a cron job runs the sync at seven fifteen, then at two fifteen.

4
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:24.248
Or, in watch mode, a small script polls Garmin every fifteen minutes, with no model at all.

5
00:00:24.348 --> 00:00:27.430
Nothing new: zero tokens spent.

6
00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:32.556
A run or a night's sleep to fetch: only then does the coach wake up.

7
00:00:33.406 --> 00:00:38.144
The coach only fetches the dates whose file is missing, and writes the Markdown.

8
00:00:38.244 --> 00:00:40.606
It ends with a five-line summary.

9
00:00:41.456 --> 00:00:46.098
That summary lands on the phone as a push notification, through ntfy.

10
00:00:46.198 --> 00:00:51.512
Sleep, heart rate variability, readiness: it's all there before the coffee.

11
00:00:52.362 --> 00:00:57.244
Want to know more? The Claude app connects to the coach machine through Remote Control.

12
00:00:57.344 --> 00:01:05.418
Camille types /today, /why, /week or /race: short answers that never change the plan.

13
00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:12.590
The dashboard reads fine on a phone too: today's verdict, the week, and the coach's approval card.

14
00:01:14.268 --> 00:01:22.294
Let's be honest. No home-made app can use your subscription: only an API key, billed per token, allows it.

15
00:01:22.394 --> 00:01:26.244
Sending shoe photos from the phone is not validated.

16
00:01:26.344 --> 00:01:29.882
And if the machine goes off, there is plan B in the cloud.

17
00:01:32.432 --> 00:01:37.098
To go further: the “Coach in your pocket” page of the documentation.
